# Lifecycle scripts from dependencies are OFF.
#
# WHY: a Cloudflare Pages build runs `npm install` with NO lockfile — the CMS
# (admin/src/backend/version.ts) and the broker's fan-out both delete
# package-lock.json on purpose, because `npm ci` refuses to install when a stale
# lock disagrees with package.json and that was silently bricking updated sites.
# No lockfile means no integrity hashes, so the resolved transitive tree is
# whatever the registry serves at build time. Direct dependencies are pinned exact
# (see package.json — no `^`, no `~`), but transitives are not, and a compromised
# transitive with a `postinstall` runs arbitrary code inside the build, with the
# build environment's secrets and write access to what gets deployed. Turning
# lifecycle scripts off removes that path without needing to trust the tree.
#
# VERIFIED SAFE for this build chain, 2026-07-26: `npm install` of lanza-site
# 0.1.10 with this setting resolves the platform binaries as ordinary optional
# dependencies (@esbuild/linux-x64, @img/sharp-linux-x64, @img/sharp-libvips-linux-x64),
# and a full `lanza build` completes — 15 pages. sharp encodes, esbuild transforms,
# and the esbuild CLI shim runs. Their install scripts are optimizations, not
# requirements. Re-check this if a dependency with a real postinstall is ever added.
#
# ⚠️  PUBLISHING: this setting also suppresses OUR OWN lifecycle scripts, including
# `prepack` (which rebuilds the admin SPA into public/admin). Publishing with it
# active would ship a stale CMS. Publish with the override:
#
#     npm publish --ignore-scripts=false --otp=<code>
#
# TODO.md's publish checklist step 2 (verify the tarball) is what catches it if
# this is forgotten.
ignore-scripts=true
